      "message": "What about doing it the other way around and have an \"extractor\" allowing `assert_eq` directly in the test?\n\ne.g.\n```\n#[derive(Eq, Debug)]\nstruct FdtNodeProps {\n    compatible: Option\u003c\u0026\u0027static CStr\u003e,\n    reg: Option\u003cVec\u003cu32\u003e\u003e,\n    interrupts: Option\u003cVec\u003cu32\u003e\u003e,\n    iommus: Option\u003cVec\u003cu32\u003e\u003e,\n}\n\nimpl FdtNodeProps {\n    fn from_fdt(fdt: \u0026Fdt, path: \u0026CStr) -\u003e Option\u003cSelf\u003e {\n        let node \u003d fdt.node(path).unwrap()?;\n        let compatible \u003d ...;\n        let reg \u003d ...;\n        let interrupts \u003d ...;\n        let iommus \u003d ...;\n        Ok(Self { ... })\n    }\n}\n```\nand, in tests:\n```\nlet expected_nodes \u003d [ // You could also use a HashMap for this\n    (\n        cstr!(\"/...\"),\n        FdtNodeProps { ... },\n    ),\n    ...\n];\n\nfor (path, expected) in expected_nodes {\n    let values \u003d FdtNodeValues::from_fdt(fdt, path);\n    assert_eq!(values, Some(expected)); // if possible, also log {path}\n}\n```\n\nThe test could first extract (and validate) the IOMMU ID-to-phandle separately.",
